Discriminating an infectious collapse from a physiological one in an orchard requires the pathologist to test two explanations that converge on identical wilting symptoms yet diverge in mechanism, in the spatial pattern of death they produce, and in the laboratory evidence each leaves behind. Phytophthora cinnamomi is a soil-borne pathogen that causes root and collar rot in orchard trees, whereas the rival explanation turns on the physical condition of the soil rather than on any organism attacking the tree. {{S1}}

Field diagnosis proceeds from the crown outward. Excavating the feeder roots of affected trees is a standard step in diagnosing orchard dieback, and it is carried out before any tissue is placed on a culture medium. In contrast to the oxygen starvation of a waterlogged rhizosphere, Phytophthora collar infections show a sharp margin between discoloured necrotic cambium and healthy tissue, so the pathologist cuts into the bark at the collar and reads that boundary directly. {{S2}}

The laboratory results for this orchard admit of only one of two readings. Either root and soil samples from the affected block contained no Phytophthora propagules, or Phytophthora cinnamomi was recovered from root samples taken in the affected block; one of the two is the case, and they are not both the case. Instrumentation settles the physiological side of the question: tensiometers and soil moisture sensors record how long the root zone remained at or above saturation, and the traces from this block show that the root zone remained saturated for extended periods after the irrigation change. At least one of two conditions therefore obtained in the affected block — prolonged saturation of the root zone, or a soil-borne pathogen present in the root zone of the affected trees — and perhaps both held together.

The decisive discrimination comes from mapping the dead trees onto the topography of the block. The extended saturation of the root zone caused the distribution of tree death that the survey recorded, whereas the presence of a soil-borne pathogen in the root zone of those trees carries no comparable topographic signature. {{S3}} Where the field pattern and the irrigation record converge in this way, they govern the final diagnosis.

A rupture investigation of this kind turns on the physical record preserved in the vessel wall rather than on the competing narratives advanced by the parties. The first task is to establish where the crack began and what condition the metal was in at that place. {{S1}}

{{S2}} Chevron markings on a fracture surface point away from the origin of the crack. That outward sense cannot be squared with their convergence back along the crack path toward the initiation point; a single fracture face cannot carry both directional readings, so this question must be settled before any conclusion about initiation is drawn.

{{S3}} Whereas the fabrication radiographs fix the state of the seams only as they stood at the moment of acceptance, liquid dye penetrant testing detects subsurface lack-of-fusion flaws within a weld. Because the heat-treatment condition revealed by the hardness traverse governs the toughness of the seam metal, it dictates the critical flaw size against which the measured flaw must be judged in the assessment described next. {{S4}}

The pressure history supplies the other half of the analysis. Either the recorded internal pressure stayed below the vessel's maximum allowable working pressure throughout the upset, or it exceeded that maximum during the upset; one of those two must be the case, and they cannot both be. The flaw-size comparison just described indicates that the longitudinal seam weld contained an unrepaired fabrication flaw at the time the vessel entered service. At least one of two conditions must hold — the unrepaired flaw in the seam, or operation beyond the design envelope — and conceivably both hold together. Where the pressure trace records an excursion above the maximum allowable working pressure, that record shows that the vessel was operated at a pressure above its design envelope.

Apportionment of responsibility then follows from which of those conditions the metallurgical and instrumental records actually support, and from the extent to which each contributed to the loss of containment. Neither party's account is accepted on its own terms; each is tested against the fracture, the fabrication documentation and the process data together.

Forensic reconstruction of a structural failure begins with the physical and documentary record of the hours that precede it. The pedestrian bridge at Florida International University collapsed on March 15, 2018. The casualty record admits exactly one of two accounts, with no middle ground: either six people were killed in the collapse, or no one was killed in it. {{S1}}

{{S2}} Analysis of this kind is referenced to as-built geometry rather than to the design drawings alone, because the governing question is always whether a member was asked to carry more than it was capable of carrying; it is that comparison of demand against delivered capacity which assigns responsibility either to the drawings or to their execution. {{S3}}

The recovered concrete cores tested below the specified 28-day compressive strength, a result that indicates the design firm was right to locate the failure in deficient construction workmanship. By contrast, the construction contractor attributed the failure to errors in the structural design. Each side, in other words, pressed the explanation that lay outside its own scope of work, and the dispute narrowed to the condition of the critical nodal region where the diagonal met the deck.

Resolving such a disagreement requires asking what, precisely, was wrong with that node. At least one of two things was the case, and perhaps both: the design of the critical nodal region was deficient, or the nodal region as built departed from the project specifications. The first possibility would place the fault in the calculations that sized the connection; the second would place it in the hands that formed and cast it.

The investigation attributed the collapse to improper compaction of concrete by the contractor, a conclusion that the sub-strength cores drawn from the span confirm. That attribution, however, cannot be squared with the finding of the authoritative body: the National Transportation Safety Board determined that the probable cause of the collapse was load and capacity calculation errors made by the bridge design firm, and if those calculation errors were the probable cause, the compaction explanation is ruled out. The Board's determination is, in turn, what settles the first branch of the alternative set out above, showing the design of the critical nodal region to have been deficient.

Adjudicating between genuine instructional improvement and the rival accounts of selective enrollment and coaching toward the examination is a problem of research design rather than of rhetoric. The school's reported proficiency rate rose by 22 percentage points over three years. Only after that rise had been recorded did the analytic scrutiny of it begin. {{S1}}

It is precisely the juxtaposition of a high-stakes instrument with a low-stakes one that has produced the field's standard diagnostic: divergence between high-stakes and low-stakes score trends is treated as evidence of score inflation. Score gains appearing only on the state accountability test are regarded as strong evidence of genuine learning, and that evaluative stance and the inflation diagnostic cannot both be true. Gains produced by test preparation transfer fully to independently constructed assessments of the same subject, and such complete transfer cannot be reconciled with any inference from divergence to inflation.

{{S2}}

{{S3}} That gains cluster on the content and formats that appear most frequently on the examination is itself the clearest indication that the range of material covered in lessons has contracted.

The composition of the student body requires a different line of attack. Either every applicant to the school was offered admission, or some applicants to the school were denied admission; one of these must hold, and not both. A school that extended an offer to every single applicant could not also supply randomized groups of admitted and non-admitted applicants, since universal admission and lottery-based comparison exclude one another; it is the rejection of some applicants that brings such groups into being. {{S4}} Only when audit-test comparisons, item-level patterning, longitudinal follow-up and lottery-based estimates converge does attribution of the rise to instruction become secure.

Adjudication between the institutional and the normative versions of the democratic peace has always been a quarrel about mechanism rather than about pattern, and the terms of that quarrel were fixed early. {{S1}} Everything since has depended on how the rival mechanisms are specified. {{S2}}

Each mechanism can be restated as a claim about the actors whose conduct it purports to govern. The institutional constraint, that is to say, amounts to the proposition that democratic leaders face removal from office by voters for costly military failures; the normative mechanism, in other words, amounts to the proposition that democratic publics hold a normative aversion to using force against fellow democracies. At least one of the two is at work in democratic dyads, electoral punishment for costly wars or popular distaste for striking fellow democracies, and perhaps both are.

The observational record is where the two accounts were first expected to part company. Either standard conflict datasets record zero interstate wars between two mature democracies, or they record at least one such war; exactly one of those tallies can be correct and not both, which is why the coding of regime thresholds and battle-death cutoffs has attracted such scrutiny. Even granting the null tally of wars between mature democracies, the inference from it to regime type has been resisted from outside the research program. {{S3}}

The mechanism-level literature has produced the sharpest tests of the two logics. {{S4}} Rosato concluded that the institutional logic of democratic peace was validated and that only the normative logic failed. That conclusion and the verdict that neither the institutional nor the normative pathway finds empirical support cannot both be true, and the discrepancy has organized much of the subsequent reply literature. Tomz and Weeks found that respondents were equally willing to endorse strikes against democratic and nondemocratic targets, a result that cannot be squared with a reduced public appetite for force against democratic adversaries. The balance therefore now rests on individual-level evidence about accountability and aversion rather than on dyadic war counts alone.

When an operator and a fabricator advance opposing accounts of a cracked pressure vessel weld, the dispute is settled on the physical record of the joint rather than on the plausibility of either narrative. Fractographic examination of the fracture surface reveals beach marks characteristic of progressive fatigue crack growth. Chevron markings on a fracture surface are a definitive indicator of fatigue crack growth. Taken together, the beach marks and the chevron pattern show that the crack grew progressively under repeated pressure cycles in service; in other words, the flaw extended incrementally as a result of cyclic loading during operation.

Surface morphology, however, settles only the mechanism of growth and not the origin of the crack within the joint, and it is the origin that separates a flaw present from the moment of welding from one nucleated in a sound profile by service stresses. {{S1}}

The documentary record is interrogated in parallel with the metallurgy, since the condition of the seam when it entered service governs how the growth history must be read. {{S2}} {{S3}}

After those fabrication-stage films were taken, a successful hydrostatic proof test at commissioning demonstrates that the weld contains no fabrication defects. Destructive examination of the failed seam established that the weld contained a lack-of-fusion flaw left at fabrication, and a joint certified defect-free by proof test cannot be squared with a joint carrying an unfused root at that same location, so one of the two findings must give way. The operating history records that the vessel was operated through more pressure cycles than its design fatigue life allowed; at least one of these two conditions obtained in this case, the unfused root left by the fabricator or the exhaustion of the design cycle allowance by the operator, and perhaps both did. Because the unfused root was present in the joint as welded, the review board determined that the crack initiated at an undetected fabrication flaw rather than at a service-induced initiation site; the cycle overrun, by contrast, told against the operator's stewardship of the vessel without bearing on the question of where the crack began. The metallurgical and documentary records thus converge, and responsibility follows from the location of the origin rather than from the vigour with which either party argues.

Adjudicating between descent and contact is a matter of asking which hypothesis accounts for the fine detail of the shared material, since resemblance by itself is never decisive. The comparative method establishes common descent by demonstrating systematic sound correspondences between languages; in other words, regular sound correspondences recurring across many lexical items are the primary evidence linguists accept for genetic relationship.

{{S1}} Phonological chronology supplies a further diagnostic. A word borrowed after a sound change has ceased to operate does not display the effects of that change; that is, a loanword that postdates a sound law fails to exhibit the reflex that the law produced. This has a direct and observable consequence for the shape such words take. {{S2}}

Shared material, however, need not point to shared ancestry at all. The term Sprachbund designates a set of geographically contiguous languages that share structural features without descending from a recent common ancestor. That such configurations are real is shown by the Balkans, where Albanian, Romanian, and Bulgarian each belong to a different branch of Indo-European, a distribution that rules out any account deriving the group as a whole from one recent shared branch. {{S3}}

Documented historical bilingualism between two speech communities supports a contact explanation for a shared feature, and the centuries of bilingualism recorded across Balkan communities are what establish the article-placement pattern described just above. The dispute is thus resolved by the convergence of criteria rather than by any one of them: reconstructed correspondences that hold across the lexicon without residue favour descent, while irregular phonological shape, a tight areal distribution, and an attested history of bilingual speakers favour transmission across a language boundary.

When liability turns on whether the defendant's own conduct or an independent intervening event produced the harm, a court begins with the allocation of proof, since the party who must persuade the finder of fact on causation ordinarily determines how the dispute ends. A plaintiff must prove causation beyond a reasonable doubt in a negligence action. That requirement cannot be squared with the rule that the plaintiff bears the burden of proving causation by a preponderance of the evidence; no single claim can be governed by both measures of persuasion at once. {{S1}} {{S2}}

Any intervening act by a third party automatically relieves the original defendant of liability; that proposition cannot be reconciled with the principle that a foreseeable intervening cause leaves the chain of proximate causation intact, and a court cannot apply the two together. Courts accordingly disentangle the competing strands by asking whether the supervening event fell within the range of hazards that the defendant's carelessness created, and by scrutinising the quality of the proof offered on each side of that question. {{S3}}

Contemporaneous maintenance records document the condition of the premises before the injury occurred; the timestamped surveillance footage picks up subsequently, fixing the sequence of events immediately preceding the injury. That footage indicates that the intervening storm contributed to the plaintiff's injury, and the maintenance records show that the defendant's neglected maintenance contributed to the injury as well. At least one of those two forces, the storm or the neglected upkeep, and perhaps both of them together, brought the plaintiff's harm about.

On such a record the finder of fact must choose between two mutually exclusive dispositions: either the verdict assigned the defendant a share of the fault for the injury, or the verdict assigned the defendant no share of the fault for the injury, and exactly one of those results obtains. What proves decisive, in the end, is the documentary and recorded material that fixes both the physical condition of the place and the timing of what happened there, since records of upkeep and time-stamped imagery, tested for reliability under the governing standards of admissibility, leave the tribunal with a concrete chronology in place of competing hypotheses.

The resolution of the Cretaceous–Paleogene controversy came from geochemistry, mineralogy and geophysics in turn, each line of work narrowing the field of plausible killers. {{S1}} In the decade following that 1980 iridium report, and in sharp contrast to a lava province confined to one corner of a single subcontinent, field geologists traced boundary minerals across continents and ocean basins. {{S2}}

The provenance of those grains became the crux of the dispute, since high-pressure minerals are not readily made by the quiet effusion of lava. Because the western Indian flood basalts were emplaced through deep crustal fissures, those Deccan fissure eruptions produced the shocked quartz found at the boundary. The search for a source crater proceeded by an altogether different route, through the archives of the oil industry. {{S3}} Its dimensions, however, admit only one of two answers: either the structure is more than 100 kilometres in diameter or it is less than 100 kilometres in diameter, one or the other and not both. Its concealment beneath the peninsula and the adjoining floor of the Gulf of Mexico indicates that the impactor itself was about 1 kilometre in diameter.

Only after that buried anomaly had been mapped in the petroleum surveys did laboratory geochronology place the competing events in time. {{S4}} Because the impacting body was about a kilometre across, the Chicxulub impact injected sulfate aerosols into the stratosphere, and Deccan Traps volcanism released large volumes of sulfur dioxide into the atmosphere; at least one of those two sulfur loadings, and perhaps both, produced the darkness and cooling registered by marine microfossils. It is the convergence of mineralogical, geophysical and radiometric results upon a single globally traceable layer, rather than any one observation, that carried the argument for most workers in the field.

Sociologists approach the neighborhood question as a problem of causal identification rather than of portraiture, and the field's answer has been assembled out of successive designs, each imposing a stricter standard on how a family came to live where it lives. The earliest generation of this work was devoted to measuring the social properties of places and relating them to outcomes recorded in those same places. {{S1}}

Patrick Sharkey has documented multigenerational exposure to high-poverty neighborhoods among Black American families. That pattern of inherited place indicates that observational neighborhood studies are vulnerable to omitted variable bias from unmeasured parental traits, since the same families reappear in the same disadvantaged settings generation after generation. Precisely because unmeasured parental traits distort such estimates, Wodtke, Harding, and Elwert applied marginal structural models with inverse probability weighting to address time-varying confounding of neighborhood exposure. By contrast, sibling fixed-effects designs compare siblings whose childhoods were spent in neighborhoods of differing quality. Because every comparison is drawn within a single family, that design removes all unobserved confounding, including factors that change over time within a family.

Housing policy supplied leverage of a different kind, since the allocation of a scarce benefit by lottery converts the question of selection into a question of who was offered a chance to move. The Gautreaux program relocated Chicago public housing residents under a court-ordered desegregation remedy, and the Moving to Opportunity demonstration was mounted afterward. {{S2}} {{S3}}

The administrative details of that demonstration govern what can be inferred from it. Moving to Opportunity recruited families in all fifty states through a national lottery, a recruitment arrangement that cannot be squared with the confinement of randomized voucher offers to five metropolitan areas. Its enrollment structure admits exactly one of two possibilities: either the demonstration included a randomly assigned control group that received no new housing voucher, or every family enrolled in Moving to Opportunity received an experimental housing voucher; one of the two held, and they cannot both have held. Adjudication therefore turns on a single criterion, namely whether a given design makes neighborhood exposure independent of the family characteristics that would otherwise produce the outcome under study.

Establishing why the subpolar North Atlantic freshened was, from the outset, an attribution problem rather than a detection problem. Salinity in the upper subpolar North Atlantic declined during the 2010s; in other words, the upper waters of the subpolar North Atlantic became less salty over the course of the 2010s. Repeat hydrographic sections, Argo float profiles and moored sensors agreed on the magnitude and the vertical structure of the anomaly, which left its origin to be settled by freshwater budgets and circulation measurements rather than by salinity records alone.

The first line of inquiry was the regional freshwater budget. Over recent decades, at least one of two inputs grew, and perhaps both: Greenland meltwater runoff into the North Atlantic increased, and freshwater export from the Arctic through Fram Strait increased. The strongest evidence bearing on the Greenland term comes from satellite gravimetry and from the ice sheet's mass budget. {{S1}}

The competing explanation, internal variability of the ocean circulation itself, could be assessed only with sustained transport measurements. The RAPID-MOCHA mooring array has monitored the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation at 26.5°N since 2004, yielding a continuous subtropical transport record against which anomalies could be judged. {{S2}}

Interpreting the subpolar records in turn requires close attention to the atmospheric forcing of wintertime mixing. {{S3}} Such mixing events are the proximate mechanism by which the atmosphere imprints itself on the density structure of the subpolar gyre, and they are therefore the natural place to look for a circulation signature of the freshening.

The Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation collapsed completely during the period of observed freshening. The IPCC Sixth Assessment Report concluded that the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation did not collapse during the twentieth and early twenty-first centuries, a finding that cannot be squared with a complete shutdown in those same years. The depth reached by convection in that winter is itself evidence that the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation continued to operate throughout the period of observed freshening; either the circulation collapsed outright in those decades or it went on working through them, exactly one of the two being the case and both together impossible. What settled the matter, then, was not the salinity anomaly but the transport and convection records that accompanied it.

The dispute turned on how a single photometric offset was to be read. Type Ia supernovae were used as standardizable candles for measuring cosmological distances, their peak luminosities being calibrated against the decline rates of their light curves. Type Ia supernovae arise from the core collapse of massive stars. Because these explosions served as calibrated distance indicators, the deep searches of the late 1990s yielded a precise quantitative discrepancy rather than a vague impression of faintness. {{S1}}

That shortfall in brightness is what gave rise to the grey dust hypothesis, which posited dust grains large enough to dim light without reddening it. Put differently, part of the observed dimming of distant supernovae is caused by intervening dust. By contrast with the 1998 announcement of acceleration, which required a new repulsive component in the cosmic energy budget, the dust proposal asked for nothing more exotic than grains expelled from young galaxies. Part of the observed dimming of distant supernovae is caused by accelerated cosmic expansion; at least one of these two mechanisms must be operating, and perhaps both are.

Discriminating between them required objects far enough away that the two effects diverge, since absorption by dust accumulates monotonically with path length while the expansion history does not. Astronomers observed the supernova SN 1997ff with the Hubble Space Telescope, recovering it from repeated imaging of the Hubble Deep Field. SN 1997ff was measured at a redshift of 0.5. Its photometry cannot be squared with the claim that intervening dust is responsible for part of the observed dimming of distant supernovae. {{S2}}

{{S3}} That reversal indicates that the present-day deceleration parameter of the universe is negative. The parameter is either negative or else zero or positive; one of these two descriptions must be correct and not the other. On the strength of the high-redshift record, grey dust survives only as a minor correction to the distance scale rather than as a rival account of it.

Adjudicating the Tasaday case required anthropologists to separate the circumstances under which the group was made famous from the question of its actual antiquity. Manuel Elizalde Jr. headed PANAMIN, the Philippine government body for national minorities, at the time the Tasaday were publicized, and his office controlled both physical access to the forest and the terms in which the group was described to the world. {{S1}}

National Geographic published a feature article on the Tasaday in 1972, and that publication resulted in press and television coverage that overstated their degree of isolation from neighboring communities. The image of a people untouched since the Stone Age, once in wide circulation, proved extremely difficult for later field workers to dislodge, since every subsequent observation was measured against it rather than against the ordinary ethnography of upland Mindanao.

The Tasaday area was closed to outside researchers and journalists from the mid-1970s until 1986. After that long interval of inaccessibility had ended, Swiss journalist Oswald Iten visited the Tasaday in 1986 and reported finding them living in houses and wearing manufactured clothing. What he encountered on that visit produced the account he then put into print. {{S2}}

The controversy that followed was argued out largely within the discipline, and it turned on what the performance of cave-dwelling implied about the people performing it. Anthropologist Gerald Berreman publicly argued that the Tasaday had been staged for the benefit of outsiders, and his analysis of the publicity record shows that the Tasaday were coached by outsiders to present themselves as cave dwellers to visiting journalists. Although Berreman's indictment was pressed with considerable force, the decisive material came from historical linguistics rather than from the scrutiny of photographs, interviews and press releases. {{S3}} In other words, the Tasaday community existed independently of any staged deception, a conclusion that cannot both be true alongside the coaching of the group to perform cave-dwelling for journalists. The dispute was thus resolved not by vindicating either camp wholesale, but by distinguishing a genuine forest community of long standing from the theatrical framing imposed upon it by its patrons and its publicists.

J Harlen Bretz published his first papers on the Channeled Scabland in 1923. Bretz's flood interpretation was accepted by the geological community immediately after his 1923 publications. Yet Bretz's critics attributed the scablands to slow erosion by ordinary rivers and glacial meltwater, and a discipline that greeted the flood reading with instant assent cannot also have been a discipline arguing for patient fluvial and meltwater work: the two pictures of the reception cannot both be true. Whereas the critics insisted on gradual excavation by ordinary rivers and meltwater, Bretz defended his flood hypothesis before the Geological Society of Washington in January 1927, four years after those first papers had appeared.

The weakness of the flood case, as its opponents pressed it, was the absence of a reservoir large enough to deliver the water, and that weakness was remedied only in the decades following the 1927 defense. {{S1}}

The glacier responsible for holding that lake back has been identified in mutually exclusive ways. The Okanogan lobe of the ice sheet dammed the Clark Fork River to impound Glacial Lake Missoula. Assigning the barrier to that lobe cannot be squared with the account of the damming ice set out next; at most one of the two placements can stand. {{S2}} {{S3}}

Erratic boulders stranded hundreds of feet above valley floors record the depth of the floodwaters, and their perched positions high on the valley walls confirm that water passed through the channels at the estimated peak rate. Rhythmite layers in the Touchet beds of the Walla Walla valley record dozens of separate flood pulses.

Those boulders, left far above any level a river could reach, show that Bretz's catastrophic flood hypothesis was vindicated; in other words, the scientific community came to accept Bretz's flood origin for the Channeled Scablands. The adjudication was in the end geomorphic rather than rhetorical, decided by quantities that only a flood of the inferred scale could have produced.

The dispute begins from a datum that both parties accept. The original effect was not detected in the replication study; that is, the replication study produced no evidence of the effect reported in the original report. What is contested is not this outcome but its diagnosis, and psychologists have assembled a mixture of empirical, procedural and statistical resources for settling the question.

Logically, at least one of two things must hold of such a case, and perhaps both. The effect reported in the original study was inflated by selective reporting of analyses, or the replication procedure departed from the original procedure in a theoretically relevant respect. Whereas the first diagnosis locates the fault in the original record and its reporting practices, the second locates it in the conditions under which the effect was sought: the hidden moderator hypothesis attributes replication failure to unmeasured contextual differences between the original and replication settings. In other words, to say that the replication departed from the original in a theoretically relevant respect is to put the hidden moderator's claim in procedural terms. Adjudication therefore turns on evidence about how much setting actually matters, and here the hidden-moderator account cannot be squared with what the largest multi-site projects have measured. {{S1}}

A second line of adjudication is procedural rather than empirical. Daniel Kahneman proposed a "replication etiquette" obliging replicators to consult the authors of the original study, and that proposal resulted in the editorial machinery of Registered Replication Reports. {{S2}}

Statistical criteria supply a third resource, since the two diagnoses differ in what they predict about the magnitude of the effect. The small telescopes approach tests whether the true effect is too small to have been detectable by the original study's sample size; put differently, equivalence testing permits researchers to conclude that an effect is smaller than a prespecified bound. A replication that licenses such a conclusion shifts the burden onto the proponent of a moderator to name it and measure it.

The practical force of these conventions is clearest in disputed cases. {{S3}} The general lesson is that the choice between a nonexistent effect and an unfaithful reproduction stops being a matter of competing assertion only when protocols are fixed and vetted in advance, when putative moderators are measured rather than merely invoked, and when the smallest effect the original design could have detected is stated explicitly.

The Federalist Papers appeared in New York newspapers in 1787 and 1788 under the pseudonym "Publius". Twelve of the Federalist essays were claimed on behalf of both Alexander Hamilton and James Madison. The number of Federalist essays whose authorship was disputed is eighty-five, a tally that cannot be squared with a contested group of twelve, since the two counts of the disputed essays cannot both be right. Only after the series had run its course in the newspapers did either man commit an account of the division of labor to paper. {{S1}}

That one of the principals should have had to furnish an enumeration of his own, three decades after publication, shows why the documentary record is where the adjudication of a contested attribution begins, and why what that record yields must be set beside the evidence of the writing itself. {{S2}}

{{S3}} Such a divergence of unreflective habit between the two men confirms that what the measurement of style captures is countable and lies largely below the threshold of an author's conscious choice.

Stylometric word-frequency analysis was invented by Mosteller and Wallace in 1964. Although the two claimants worked side by side on the series in a political idiom they held in common, the twelve contested essays were nonetheless submitted to statistical discrimination. {{S4}}

The outcome of that analysis indicates that Madison wrote all twelve of the disputed Federalist essays. Hamilton, for his part, wrote at least one of the twelve; exactly one of these two outcomes can be the true one, for one of them must hold and the two cannot hold together. A finding of that kind — quantitative, replicable, and owing nothing to the testimony of interested parties — is what brings a disputed attribution to rest.

Adjudicating between genuine generalization and contamination of the training data is a matter of designing evaluations whose outcomes come apart under the two hypotheses. The first family of methods is behavioural. Researchers construct a fresh test set whose items were written after the model's training cutoff date. Scale AI's release of GSM1k as a held-out benchmark reconstructing the distribution of GSM8K shows that such post-cutoff construction can indeed be carried out at scale and at matched difficulty.

{{S1}}

{{S2}} After that release of GSM1k, the model's accuracy fell by 13 percentage points on the newly authored problems, a gap of the size one expects when familiar items have been seen rather than solved. By contrast, the headline result of the GSM1k study was that GPT-4 suffered the largest accuracy drop among all evaluated models, which shifts the interpretive burden onto the strongest systems rather than the weakest.

The second family of methods works on the data and the model itself rather than on aggregate scores, and its value depends on what an external auditor is actually permitted to examine. {{S3}}

{{S4}} The impossibility of auditing a sealed corpus from the outside has therefore led directly to statistics computed from the model alone: Min-K% Prob scores a sequence using the probabilities of its least likely tokens. Min-K% Prob requires access to the model's full training corpus to compute its score. That corpus requirement cannot be squared with a quantity read off the lowest-probability tokens of the candidate sequence, and the practice of membership inference described above rules it out as well, since estimating whether a particular example was seen is undertaken precisely where the corpus cannot be consulted at all. Converging verdicts from post-cutoff retesting, variant generation and model-side detection are what finally settle the question for any given system.

Mechanistic disputes of this kind turn on a single structural question about the potential energy surface. A concerted mechanism passes through a single transition state connecting reactants directly to products, whereas a stepwise mechanism contains a local energy minimum corresponding to a discrete intermediate. Deciding between the two therefore requires experiments whose outcome depends on whether that minimum is ever occupied.

The most informative observations are usually stereochemical. In the cycloaddition of a cis- or trans-disubstituted alkene, the relative configuration of the substituents in the starting alkene is preserved in the cycloadduct; in other words, the product retains the stereochemistry present in the alkene substrate. Because passage from reactants to products across one transition state leaves no interval in which the substrate geometry can relax, that single-transition-state description makes the stereochemical result of such an experiment diagnostic of the pathway. {{S1}}

That conclusion has exactly one rival, and the two cannot both hold while one of them must: either the reaction coordinate is free of any such species, or at least one discrete intermediate lies between reactants and products on the reaction coordinate. What decides for the second case is loss of stereochemical integrity, since scrambling of substituent stereochemistry in the product is consistent with an intermediate whose bonds can rotate before closure, and the observation of such scrambling indicates that an intermediate does intervene. At least one of the following holds, perhaps both: a diradical species exists as an energy minimum along the reaction coordinate, and a zwitterionic species exists as an energy minimum along the reaction coordinate. The characterization of the diradical as a genuine well on the surface confirms that an intermediate is interposed between reactants and products.

Kinetic and isotopic measurements supply complementary information about the geometry of bond formation. {{S2}} A further class of experiments addresses whether the reacting partners remain bound to one another throughout the transformation. {{S3}}

A dispute of this sort is ultimately settled not by any single measurement but by convergence: one mechanistic description must accommodate the stereochemical outcome, the magnitude and distribution of isotope effects, the labelling results, the solvent and substituent dependences, and the computed energy surface, while its rival is driven to increasingly contrived auxiliary suppositions. When high-level calculations locate, or fail to locate, the stationary point that the stepwise account demands, and when direct spectroscopic or ultrafast detection either captures the putative intermediate or places an upper bound on its lifetime shorter than a vibrational period, the question ceases to be open and the community adopts the surviving description.

Where an operator and a fabricator each lay the rupture of a pressure vessel at the other's door, the dispute is resolved not by the parties' assertions but by the physical record left in the fractured metal, which preserves the sequence of events in a form neither side can edit. The analyst's task is therefore to read the fracture surface, the service pressure history and the construction record as mutually constraining bodies of evidence. {{S1}}

{{S2}} Concentric beach marks around the initiation site are themselves the product of a single overload rupture, and that cannot be squared with treating beach marking as the register of crack extension accumulated cycle by cycle, for one band pattern on one surface cannot carry both meanings. At finer scale, fatigue striations on the fracture face are resolved using a scanning electron microscope. Fatigue striations on a steel fracture surface are visible to the unaided eye, and these two accounts of their scale cannot both be true, since a feature brought out only by electron optics is not one the naked eye picks out across a ruptured shell.

Code conformance in the vessel's manufacture notwithstanding, the fracture origin was located at the toe of the circumferential seam weld; the radiographic record of the root condition of that same seam confirms that the separation began at that location. {{S3}}

Only after the condition of the weld root had been set down in the fabrication record did the inquiry turn to the vessel's service pressure history. {{S4}} Crack-growth-rate calculations run backwards from the measured striation spacing then fix the interval over which the flaw extended to critical dimensions, and the two timelines are laid side by side. That comparison against the design cycle life supplies the evidence for the tribunal's holding that the rupture resulted from progressive fatigue cracking initiating at a fabrication defect, a determination returned after the pressurization history had been reconstructed from the recorder traces.

Prevailing medical opinion in the 1970s attributed peptic ulcers to stress and excess gastric acid secretion. That attribution resulted directly from the fact that the stomach was widely believed to be too acidic for bacteria to colonize: since no organism was thought capable of surviving in gastric juice, the cause of ulceration had to be sought in secretory physiology and in the patient's nervous state, and therapy was aimed accordingly at neutralizing or suppressing acid.

The first crack in that framework was histological. Robin Warren observed curved bacteria in gastric biopsy specimens at Royal Perth Hospital in 1979. Only afterwards, in 1982, was the bacterium first successfully cultured, the breakthrough arriving when culture plates were left to incubate for five days over an Easter holiday. That success in the laboratory led Warren and Marshall to publish letters in The Lancet in 1983 describing unidentified curved bacilli in the human stomach.

The organism's persistence in so hostile an environment has a straightforward biochemical explanation: Helicobacter pylori produces urease and generates ammonia that buffers the surrounding acid. This mechanism cannot be squared with the older conviction that gastric acidity precluded bacterial colonization altogether; the two pictures of the gastric niche cannot both be correct, and the urease chemistry is what rules the earlier one out. {{S1}}

What finally settled the dispute, however, was therapeutic rather than microbiological. Duodenal ulcers treated with acid suppression alone relapsed in most patients within a year of stopping the drug; the outcomes obtained once antibacterial treatment was added ran in precisely the opposite direction. {{S2}} That is to say, clearing the bacterium with antimicrobial therapy produced lasting remission of peptic ulcers. This pattern of cure, reproduced across trial populations, converted a contested hypothesis into standard gastroenterological practice, and institutional recognition followed only after that therapeutic result was firmly on record. {{S3}}

The anomaly was identified, quantified, and finally adjudicated over more than half a century of orbital bookkeeping. Urbain Le Verrier reported in 1859 that Mercury's perihelion advanced faster than Newtonian theory predicted, and that report fixed the magnitude of the discrepancy, showing that Mercury's perihelion advances by about 43 arcseconds per century beyond the Newtonian prediction. In other words, the unexplained residual in Mercury's apsidal motion amounts to roughly 43 arcseconds each century, a quantity far too large to be absorbed by the uncertainties in the adopted masses of the known planets. {{S1}}

Two readings of the residual were available, and at least one of them had to be right, perhaps both: Newtonian gravitation is an incomplete description of the Sun's gravitational field, or an undetected mass inside Mercury's orbit perturbs the planet's motion — the latter being Le Verrier's proposal restated in dynamical terms, that is, his conjectured planet described by the pull it would exert. Since the two readings made quite different observational demands, the decision turned on whether such a body could actually be found, and through the following decades observers scrutinized the Sun's neighbourhood during total eclipses and watched for transits across the solar disc. {{S2}} Such a sighting counted as direct observational evidence for the intra-Mercurial planet Le Verrier had postulated.

The residual itself was meanwhile subjected to repeated reduction, since a defect in the law of gravitation could be inferred only from a figure secure against errors in the planetary masses and in the constant of precession. {{S3}}

Three decades after Newcomb's recomputation, Einstein derived the residual precession from his field equations in a paper presented in November 1915. Einstein's derivation of the precession contained no adjustable parameters fitted to the Mercury data; the number followed from the geometry of the field surrounding a spherical mass. That 1915 derivation, by accounting for the whole residual without any perturbing body, is the evidence that no planet has ever been confirmed to orbit inside the orbit of Mercury, and this cannot be squared with the photographic recovery of Watson's eclipse object and its entry into the planetary catalogues. The anomaly was thus resolved on the side of gravitation theory rather than of celestial cartography.

When a laboratory report and a connoisseur's eye appear to point in opposite directions, the dispute is adjudicated not by ranking the two kinds of expertise but by specifying what each is competent to decide. The first step is to interrogate each physical technique for the limits built into its own procedure. Dendrochronology dates oak panel supports by matching tree-ring sequences to regional reference chronologies, and the structure of that matching procedure thereby determines exactly what such a date delivers. {{S1}}

Consider the terms in which a panel dispute is ordinarily posed. Either the oak panel came from a tree felled before 1550, or it came from a tree felled after 1550; one of these holds, and they cannot both hold. Whichever obtains narrows the field of candidate workshops without by itself naming a hand. Pigment identification supplies a second order of chronological marker. {{S2}}

With pigments, stratigraphy matters as much as chemistry, since paint can be added to a surface long after the picture itself is finished. At least one of two accounts must hold, and perhaps both: the anachronistic pigment belongs to a later restoration campaign, or the painting was executed after the master's death. In the case at hand, the attribution committee concluded that the anachronistic pigment belonged to a nineteenth-century restoration rather than the original paint layer; in other words, the offending material came from a later campaign of repair and not from the artist's own hand, a finding that rules out any claim that the painting was executed after the master's death.

{{S3}} That such a resin could be detected at all indicates the general principle on which adjudication rests: technical evidence can exclude an attribution without establishing authorship. Connoisseurial judgment, by contrast, rests on comparison with securely documented works by the artist. The two forms of expertise therefore enter the argument at different points, and a responsible committee treats a laboratory result as a constraint on the field of possible authors rather than as a verdict about hands, reserving the positive ascription for the comparative argument while declining any ascription that the materials render impossible.

Adjudicating between the two accounts requires attention to the order in which events unfolded and to the evidence each side treats as decisive. Lawrence Summers warned in February 2021 that the scale of the fiscal package risked igniting inflationary pressures; after that warning, the American Rescue Plan Act was signed in March 2021, providing roughly $1.9 trillion in fiscal support. Because much of that support arrived as transfers at a moment when opportunities to spend were curtailed, it resulted in an unusually large stock of unspent purchasing power on household balance sheets. {{S1}}

The demand-side case then runs from balance sheets to the labor market: the release of that accumulated saving as the economy reopened caused employers to compete for a pool of workers far smaller than their hiring plans required, pushing posted vacancies well above the number of job seekers. {{S2}} For the opposing camp, the December 2021 extreme in measured global supply-chain pressure is the central evidence showing that the first phase of the price increase originated in commodity markets and sectoral bottlenecks rather than in domestic spending. {{S3}}

Bernanke and Blanchard concluded that fiscal stimulus explained nearly all of the US inflation surge. The IMF's cross-country finding that larger support packages were followed by higher inflation is strong evidence for that conclusion. It cannot, however, be squared with the reading of the initial 2021 increase as mainly a matter of commodity prices and sectoral shortages: an origin in bottlenecks and an origin almost wholly in fiscal policy cannot both be true of the same episode.

Since the rival narratives both fit the aggregate price path, adjudication turns on decomposition rather than on storytelling. {{S4}} Euro area inflation remained below 3 percent throughout 2022, which confirms that nearly the whole of the American surge is attributable to fiscal stimulus, since economies exposed to the same global disruptions but far smaller packages saw nothing comparable. A final test concerns the labor market: either US unemployment remained below 5 percent throughout 2023, or it rose above 5 percent during that year, and exactly one of those two must be the case.

Adjudicating between rival accounts of a practice shared by two distant societies begins with making explicit what each account commits its proponent to. Diffusionist accounts explain a shared trait as transmission from a single point of origin through contact. Independent invention accounts, by contrast, explain a shared trait as separate responses to similar problems. {{S1}}

{{S2}} Comparison of the earliest appearances admits two outcomes, and exactly one of them must hold in any given case: either the practice is securely dated earlier in one society than in the other, or the earliest securely dated evidence of the practice is contemporaneous in both societies. Demonstrable priority in one region opens a corridor along which transmission could have run, while genuine simultaneity, in the absence of any plausible corridor, leaves parallel invention the stronger reading.

Radiocarbon chronology, however, fixes only the order in which a practice surfaces; linguistic evidence, by contrast, bears directly on the mechanism of transfer, for loanwords that accompany a practice indicate borrowing between the two societies. The same reasoning is applied to technical terminology, units of measure and ritual formulae, whose shared forms are difficult to attribute to convergent coinage.

The sweet potato in Oceania is the standard test case. The Polynesian word "kumara" resembles the Quechua word "kumar", and that resemblance shows that the sweet potato reached Polynesia through human transport across the Pacific. At least one of two mechanisms must have carried the plant into the islands, and perhaps both did: human transport across the Pacific, or natural dispersal across the ocean. The lexical correspondence, though, cannot be squared with natural dispersal across the ocean, which would have delivered the tuber without any attendant vocabulary.

The transport of the sweet potato into eastern Polynesia occurred long before the twentieth-century voyages staged to reconstruct such a crossing. {{S3}}

Adjudicating between a departing insider's deliberate exfiltration and an outsider operating with stolen credentials is ultimately a problem of attributing authenticated activity to a particular pair of hands, and the chronology usually frames that inquiry before any artifact is examined. The engineer submitted his resignation two weeks before the data transfer was discovered. {{S1}}

{{S2}} Those attachment traces are evidence that the proprietary files were copied onto a removable USB storage device, and the technical record taken as a whole establishes that at least one of two exfiltration routes was used, perhaps both: the copy to removable media, or an upload of the same files to an external cloud storage account. Each route leaves a characteristically different signature, and the examiner's task is to read whichever signatures survive.

The network record points in the opposite direction from the physical one. VPN authentication logs recorded the engineer's account authenticating from an IP address allocated to an Eastern European hosting provider; that offshore session supplies the evidential basis for the impossible-travel finding, and it cuts against what the building's own access record says about where the engineer actually was. {{S3}}

Attribution then turns on the authentication fabric itself, where the question is not where a session originated but who approved it. The multi-factor authentication provider's logs showed push approvals issued to a device never enrolled by the engineer. Two interpretive conventions govern the reading of such records: Windows Event ID 4624 with logon type 10 indicates a local console logon performed at the keyboard, and IP geolocation data identifies the street address of the device used in a remote login.

At the moment of the data transfer, either the engineer's credentials were used by the engineer himself, or they were used by a person other than the engineer; one of those two must be the case, and they cannot both be. The pairing of sessions too far apart to be bridged in the elapsed time rules out the first of those alternatives, since no single account holder can sit at two such keyboards at once, while the push approvals delivered to a handset the engineer never enrolled indicate the second. The forensic conclusion therefore rests not on a departing employee's apparent motive but on the demonstrable question of whose hands the credentials were in.

Disputes of this kind are not settled by asking which team ran the more careful study; they are settled by building replications whose structure makes the two rival explanations empirically separable. The facial feedback hypothesis supplies the clearest worked example. The original 1988 experiment enrolled more than 500 participants, and its finding stood for nearly three decades before a coordinated multi-site replication was finally mounted. {{S1}} Variation of that kind is the central diagnostic: if unmeasured local conditions carry the effect, laboratories should diverge sharply from one another, whereas if the effect is simply not there, they should converge on a null.

The outcome was unambiguous. The pooled effect estimate across the seventeen replication laboratories was indistinguishable from zero; in other words, the combined replication data showed no detectable influence of pen position on funniness ratings, and it is the material assembled by those seventeen contributing laboratories that establishes this absence. Defenders of the original result answered on procedural rather than statistical grounds. {{S2}}

The adjudication then turns on what a null of this precision licenses. Because no trace of a pen-position influence survived pooling, the evidence indicates that the original 1988 estimate of the effect was larger than the true population effect. The complaint about recording equipment amounts, restated in more general terms, to the claim that the replication sessions contained procedural features that were absent from the original 1988 sessions. At least one of these two accounts holds, and perhaps both do: either the first estimate ran ahead of reality, or the later sessions introduced something the earlier ones lacked. {{S3}}

Several further particulars belong to the record. {{S4}} Strack, by contrast, endorsed the conclusion that his 1988 finding was a false positive. The lesson is methodological. What resolves a contest between falsity and infidelity is never a declaration about who was faithful to the original design, but a chain of evidence: a pre-registered pooled estimate, a direct measure of dispersion across sites, and an experiment aimed squarely at the procedural difference alleged to matter.

Adjudicating between inheritance and contact is a matter of asking which mechanism could have produced the particular shared feature, and the diagnostics available differ in kind. The comparative method establishes genetic relationship through systematic sound correspondences across the lexicons of two languages. Antoine Meillet, by contrast, treated shared morphological irregularities as the strongest evidence of common descent, since arbitrary paradigmatic anomalies are not the sort of thing a borrowing speech community reproduces wholesale. A further diagnostic concerns the internal stratification of the lexicon. {{S1}}

The signature of contact cuts against the very regularity of correspondence on which the comparative method relies. {{S2}} Structural resemblance poses a harder problem, because what diffuses in that domain is pattern rather than substance. {{S3}}

The Hungarian case shows how such a dispute is settled on the evidence. Hungarian shares inherited basic vocabulary and verbal morphology with Khanty and Mansi, a correspondence that cannot be squared with membership in the Turkic family and that demonstrates instead that Hungarian belongs to the Uralic language family. That Uralic affiliation is itself the evidence indicating that every Turkic-looking word in Hungarian entered the language through borrowing. Only two outcomes are open here, and exactly one of them can hold: either every Turkic-looking item in the Hungarian lexicon is a loan, or at least one Turkic–Hungarian lexical resemblance is an inheritance from a common ancestor.

Within the borrowed stratum the chronology can be stratified further. At least one of the following is true, and perhaps both: some Turkic words entered Hungarian before the Magyars reached the Carpathian Basin, and some Turkic words entered Hungarian after the Magyars settled in the Carpathian Basin. The decision, in short, rests not on the bare existence of resemblance but on where in the lexicon the resemblance falls, whether it conforms to the sound laws of the recipient language, and whether it reaches into morphological paradigms.

Sociologists use the term concentrated disadvantage to describe neighborhoods with overlapping poverty, joblessness, and family disruption. Whether such contexts shape life chances, or merely assemble people whose trajectories were already fixed, has structured the field's methodological quarrels for a generation. {{S1}}

That unresolved impasse over separating context from sorting led directly to the adoption of within-family comparison as a remedy. {{S2}}

It was precisely because the determinants of residential location remain unmeasured that the discipline turned toward designs resting on chance assignment. Unlike strategies that hold fixed whatever children of the same parents have in common, this newer approach seeks its leverage in variation imposed from outside the household. {{S3}} That ambition to sever family background from neighborhood context resulted in the Moving to Opportunity demonstration, launched by the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development in 1994.

Moving to Opportunity assigned participating families to its treatment and control groups by lottery, which shows that voucher allocation can indeed serve as the identifying instrument in work of this kind. Random assignment of that sort and purely observational inference are mutually exclusive: either the demonstration allocated units by lottery, or it relied entirely on observational comparisons of volunteer families with no random assignment, and exactly one of those two descriptions can hold of it. The demonstration enrolled roughly 40,000 families across twenty American cities. Its age gradients repay close attention. Children in Moving to Opportunity families who relocated after age eighteen showed the largest adult earnings gains, a pattern that stands in sharp contrast to what the interim evaluation reported about adult labor-market outcomes.

Those interim results became available only after the 1994 launch of the demonstration. {{S4}} Adjudication between contextual and individual accounts therefore rests less on any single finding than on whether a design can credibly claim that neighborhood exposure was fixed by something other than the residents themselves.

The dispute over the fate of the core of SN 1987A begins with the progenitor itself. Sanduleak −69 202 was a red supergiant at the time of its explosion, and that explosion occurred well before any of the signals later analysed on Earth were gathered. {{S1}} The timing of the recorded burst shows that the detected neutrinos arrived over a span of roughly ten seconds; that is, the neutrino signal from the collapse lasted on the order of a dozen seconds.

Because the collapse had produced a hot proto-neutron star rather than an immediate void, the remnant's later history depended entirely on how much material rained back onto that newborn surface in the hours that followed. {{S2}}

Discriminating between an obscured neutron star and a black hole therefore meant hunting for the signature of a cooling, magnetised surface across every waveband the remnant allows, since the centre has never been resolved as a point source in visible light. {{S3}}

Two further strands of reasoning closed the argument. The reason the centre appears so feeble at optical wavelengths, and hence the reason the Hubble limits come out as tight as they do, lies in the material that has condensed between the compact object and the observer. The hard X-ray spectra, for their part, serve as evidence about what must be illuminating the innermost gas. {{S4}} The JWST detection of the central emission lines was made with the NIRCam imaging camera.

The absorbing material in the inner ejecta amounts, in other words, to a dense clump of dust and ejecta that enshrouds the compact object and blocks its direct light. At least one of two circumstances must accordingly hold of the centre, and perhaps both together: that shroud, and an intrinsically faint thermal surface emission of the compact object. On this reading the missing object is a neutron star hidden from view rather than a black hole, and the question was settled not by a direct image but by the energy budget of the gas that the object continues to ionize.

The inquiry into whether a bridge collapse originated in a design deficiency or in the owner's maintenance regime begins at the debris field, with the steel itself. {{S1}} By contrast, section loss from corrosion is not read off fracture morphology at all; it is measured directly on the recovered steel plates and chords, by gauging and by comparison with the thicknesses specified on the fabrication drawings.

The analytical half of the exercise proceeds in a fixed sequence. The original design calculations are recomputed against the loads actually present at the moment of collapse — dead load, the superimposed weight of successive resurfacings, live load and wind — and only after that recomputation is complete does the modelling stage follow. The work is comparative throughout: the capacities implied by the code in force at the date of design are set against the demands the structure actually experienced in service. {{S2}}

Maintenance history is established from the owner's own files. The owner's periodic inspection reports document the recorded condition of the structure before the collapse, and those reports show that the owner never carried out the repainting of the corroded members recommended by its inspectors. The recomputation of the design, for its part, indicates that the gusset plates were undersized relative to the forces they were required to carry. The omission of the repainting caused corrosion that had thinned the gusset plates before the day of the collapse; at least one of the two deficiencies — inadequate plate thickness as drawn, or thickness lost to rust — was operative in the failure, and perhaps both were at once.

What the event cost in human terms is a matter of record on which the tribunal must reach a finding. Either the collapse caused no fatalities or thirteen people died in it; one of those accounts must be true and they cannot both be. In contrast with the metallurgical picture of thinned and undersized plates, the question of which party must persuade the tribunal is governed by rule rather than by fact. {{S3}}

Competing explanations of an abrupt score jump are adjudicated not by weighing their plausibility in the abstract, but by deriving from each a distinct set of observable consequences and testing the forensic record against them. Genuine instructional reform predicts gains distributed across items roughly in proportion to item difficulty, visible in classroom work, and sustained in later cohorts; irregular administration predicts signatures in the answer documents themselves. {{S1}} {{S2}}

Erasure analysis measures the rate of wrong-to-right answer changes on scanned answer sheets. Lincoln Elementary's wrong-to-right erasure rate exceeded the state mean by more than five standard deviations, whereas on legitimately completed answer sheets right-to-wrong erasures outnumber wrong-to-right erasures. Erasure analysis identifies with certainty which individual student altered each answer, a property that cannot be squared with the technique's being no more than a measure of wrong-to-right change rates across a scanned document; an aggregate rate attaches to a sheet of paper, not to a culprit.

Item-level response patterns showed implausibly high accuracy on the most difficult test items, which is the trace a transcription of correct answers leaves behind rather than the profile incremental teaching gains produce. That statistical work was finished before any witness was questioned. {{S3}}

The implausibly high accuracy on the hardest items indicates that proctors supplied correct answers to students during the test session. The overnight storage of completed sheets, unsealed, in a school office resulted in school staff altering student answer sheets after the test session had ended. At least one of these two mechanisms was at work at Lincoln Elementary, answers fed to pupils in the room or sheets reworked once the room was empty, and perhaps both were. After the four teachers' accounts of the unsealed sheets, either the state invalidated at least some of Lincoln Elementary's fifth-grade test scores or no test scores at Lincoln Elementary were invalidated; exactly one of those two dispositions obtained, for they cannot both stand. The adjudication therefore turns on convergence: an instructional-reform account must explain the document-level signatures as well as the headline gain, and it cannot.

The failed unit was a 230/69 kV oil-immersed power transformer at the utility's substation. Two incompatible accounts of the event were placed on the record by the parties. The utility attributed the failure to a defect in the high-voltage winding insulation, whereas the manufacturer attributed the failure to a switching transient produced by capacitor bank operation on the adjacent feeder. Resolving a dispute of this kind is a matter of ordering the physical evidence, since the diagnostic signatures of an internally initiated fault and of an externally imposed overvoltage differ in kind. Engineers therefore begin not with the parties' assertions but with the records that the apparatus and its protection scheme generated independently of either of them.

{{S1}} Applied to oil drawn from the failed unit, that measurement is what produced the central laboratory finding of the investigation. {{S2}}

By contrast, elevated carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide levels in the oil indicate degradation of cellulose paper insulation, a signature that implicates the solid rather than the liquid portion of the insulation system. Digital fault recorders at the substation captured voltage and current waveforms during the event. Such records permit the sequence of voltage collapse and current rise to be reconstructed at sub-cycle resolution, which is the only way to establish whether the disturbance on the system preceded the collapse of dielectric strength in the apparatus or followed it. {{S3}}

After the waveform records had been secured, untanking and internal inspection located the failure site within the winding, precisely the region that the elevated carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide readings had indicated. The geometry of the damage then becomes decisive: a puncture concentrated at the line-end turns, where impulse stress accumulates, carries a different implication from a failure distributed through the body of the coil. Taken together, the oil chemistry, the recorded waveforms, the surge history at the terminals and the physical condition of the conductor and paper permit the investigator to determine which of the two competing accounts the hardware itself will support, and to discard the other.

One further matter conditions the commercial consequences of whatever technical finding is reached. Either the transformer was within its manufacturer warranty period on the date of failure, or the manufacturer's warranty had expired before the date of failure; one of those two situations must be the case, and they cannot both obtain.
